TITLE = "Opposing organic and inorganic carbon responses mask a net loss of soil carbon this century"
SHORT_TITLE = "Joint SOC–SIC vulnerability"
ONE_SENTENCE = "Climate change removes soil organic carbon faster than it adds inorganic carbon, so total soil carbon decreases this century."

STRUCTURED = [
("INTRODUCTION", "Soils hold the largest active terrestrial carbon pool, in two chemically distinct forms: soil organic carbon (SOC), derived from plant and microbial residues, and soil inorganic carbon (SIC), present mainly as pedogenic and lithogenic carbonates. Global assessments, national greenhouse-gas inventories, and soil-carbon policies are built almost exclusively on SOC, whereas SIC, which is concentrated in drylands, is usually treated as inert. A recent global synthesis showed that SIC is a pool of comparable size that is vulnerable to acidification. The two pools have opposite drivers: SOC responds directly to temperature and moisture, whereas SIC is controlled by aridity and soil pH, and nitrogen-driven acidification dissolves carbonates. How total soil carbon will respond to climate change is therefore unknown, because assessing either pool alone can misstate its net fate."),
("RATIONALE", "We built a joint, depth-resolved inventory of SOC, SIC, and the mineral-associated (MAOC) and particulate (POC) organic carbon fractions to 2 m depth. Quality-controlled WoSIS profiles (174,092 profiles) and 1005 harmonized fraction measurements were linked to climate, soil, terrain, and lithology covariates with gradient-boosted models that were evaluated by random 10-fold cross-validation. We asked whether climate change produces a trade-off between the pools (SOC loss with SIC gain) or a co-loss, distinguished where the soil carbon system would settle under a future climate (equilibrium, space-for-time) from how much of that change can be realized by 2100 (time-limited response), re-evaluated nitrogen-driven carbonate loss with a published empirical pH–nitrogen relation, and split the SOC change into its fast (POC) and slow (MAOC) components."),
("RESULTS", "Global soils store 4105 Pg of total carbon (3675 to 4549; ±265) in the upper 2 m: 2506 Pg SOC [90% confidence interval (CI), 2263 to 2761 Pg; ±151 Pg (±1 SD)] and 1598 Pg SIC (1408 to 1801; ±119), so that SIC accounts for 39% of the total and 89% of SIC lies below 30 cm. Aridity controls the partitioning between the two pools mainly through soil pH: SIC occurrence follows a sigmoid function of pH with a midpoint at pH 7.10, and the probability of carbonate presence rises steeply above pH ≈ 6.6. Under SSP5-8.5, at equilibrium SOC decreases by 378 Pg (90% CI, 254 to 526 Pg) and SIC increases by 229 Pg (130 to 382 Pg), so total carbon (TC) decreases by 150 Pg (41 to 245 Pg); SOC decreases while SIC increases on 69 Mkm² (50% of mapped land), and both decrease on 16%. With first-order turnover, only about 3% of the equilibrium SIC gain is realized by 2100 (SIC increases by 7 Pg; 2 to 19 Pg), whereas half of the SOC loss is (SOC decreases by 188 Pg; 105 to 292 Pg), so TC decreases by 180 Pg (100 to 282 Pg) by 2100. Nitrogen-driven acidification decreases SIC by 1.6 to 2.2 Pg in the topsoil and 13.4 to 17.4 Pg in the upper 2 m over 2020–2050 under IMAGE fertilizer trajectories (SSP2-4.5 interpolated), far less than previously published GLOBIOM-based topsoil losses of 10 to 23 Pg; because this central nitrogen loss exceeds the climate-driven SIC gain realized by 2100, the direction of SIC change this century is uncertain, whereas TC decreases in every case. Within topsoil SOC, particulate organic carbon (POC) decreases by 39 Pg both at equilibrium and by 2100, whereas mineral-associated organic carbon (MAOC) decreases by 37 Pg by 2100 out of a 78 Pg equilibrium loss and carries the larger committed loss beyond 2100."),
("CONCLUSION", "The net change in soil carbon is the SOC decrease minus the SIC increase. An SOC-only assessment overstates the equilibrium decrease of total soil carbon, whereas ignoring SIC understates the risk to a large, acid-sensitive pool; because carbonate gains are slow and organic losses fast, total soil carbon decreases this century and soils become a net carbon source. The split of the SOC loss between POC and MAOC determines how quickly it is expressed and how persistent it is. Joint SOC–SIC monitoring, depth-explicit inventories, nitrogen and liming management that limit acidification in carbonate-bearing drylands, and protection of fast organic pools where SOC dominates should be part of how soils are represented in climate policy."),
]

ABSTRACT = "Soils are the largest terrestrial carbon pool, yet global assessments rarely combine soil organic carbon (SOC) with soil inorganic carbon (SIC), which responds to climate in the opposite direction. Using 174,092 soil profiles and machine learning, we estimate that the top 2 m of soil stores 2506 Pg SOC (90% CI, 2263 to 2761) and 1598 Pg SIC (1408 to 1801). Under a high-emission scenario, at equilibrium SOC decreases by 378 Pg (254 to 526) and SIC increases by 229 Pg (130 to 382), so total soil carbon decreases by 150 Pg (41 to 245). Because carbonate formation is slow, only about 3% of the SIC gain is realized by 2100, when total soil carbon decreases by 180 Pg (100 to 282). Particulate organic carbon realizes its loss this century; mineral-associated carbon carries a larger committed loss."

MAIN = [
('p', "Soil carbon is the largest active carbon reservoir on land and a central term in the global carbon budget [@friedlingstein22;@lal04]. Almost all of what is known about its size and vulnerability, however, concerns soil organic carbon (SOC) [@batjes96;@sanderman17;@crowther16]. Soil inorganic carbon (SIC), mainly calcium and magnesium carbonates inherited from parent material or formed in situ through pedogenesis [@zamanian16;@monger15], dominates many dryland soils but has conventionally been regarded as inert and is usually left out of global assessments. That view is changing. Carbonates dissolve within decades when soils acidify [@kim20;@song22], nitrogen fertilization accelerates carbonate-derived CO₂ release [@zamanian18;@raza20], and a recent global synthesis estimated that 2305 ± 636 Pg of SIC is stored in the top 2 m of soil, with up to 23 Pg of topsoil SIC at risk from nitrogen-driven acidification over the next 30 years [@huang24]. Because the two pools have been assessed separately, the net fate of total soil carbon (TC = SOC + SIC) under climate change is unknown. Resolving it requires both pools in one framework."),
('p', "SOC and SIC have opposite drivers. SOC reflects the balance between plant inputs and microbial decomposition and responds directly to temperature and moisture [@crowther16;@koven17]. SIC persistence is set by soil pH, which is governed by the soil water balance [@slessarev16]: leaching in humid climates removes base cations and carbonates while favoring plant inputs and organic matter accumulation, whereas in drylands low leaching allows carbonates to persist and organic inputs are small. Nitrogen-induced acidification adds an anthropogenic driver that dissolves carbonates irrespective of climate [@zamanian18;@huang24]. A warming and, in many regions, drying climate that depletes SOC may therefore move soils toward conditions in which carbonates are favored, producing a trade-off in which one pool declines while the other grows. Where water balance or acidification shifts the other way, both pools may decline together. Which outcome prevails as climate zones shift [@beck23] determines whether SIC compensates for SOC losses; whether that compensation is realized on policy-relevant timescales is a separate question, because SOC responds within decades, whereas carbonate accumulation is slow [@zamanian16;@monger15]."),
('p', "The nature of an SOC loss matters as much as its size. Particulate organic carbon (POC) turns over in years to decades and responds quickly to climate and to changes in organic inputs, whereas mineral-associated organic carbon (MAOC) is protected by association with fine mineral particles and persists for decades to centuries [@lavallee20;@cotrufo19;@georgiou22]. Which fraction carries a loss therefore determines how quickly it is expressed and how readily it can be reversed: POC can be rebuilt by restoring inputs, whereas MAOC losses deplete the most persistent store and continue after the forcing stabilizes."),
('p', "Here we build a joint, depth-resolved inventory of SOC, SIC, MAOC, and POC and follow total soil carbon from its present partitioning, through its drivers, to its response to climate change and nitrogen-driven acidification, and finally to the organic fractions that carry the loss. We follow the digital soil-mapping framework of Huang *et al.* [@huang24] (observation screening, two-step SIC models, random 10-fold cross-validation, and the empirical nitrogen–pH relation) and extend it with organic-horizon handling, declustering, organic-carbon fractions, and an explicit time-limited response (materials and methods). Our accounting is simple: ΔTC = ΔSOC + ΔSIC, where a negative change is a decrease of the soil stock (a loss to the atmosphere or waters); under climate change ΔSOC is negative and ΔSIC positive, so the net change is the SOC decrease minus the SIC increase, and the character of the SOC decrease is set by its MAOC and POC components."),

('h', "Inorganic carbon is a large, deep, and dryland-concentrated share of soil carbon"),
('p', "We compiled soil profiles from the World Soil Information Service (WoSIS) [@batjes20] and screened them following Huang *et al.* [@huang24] by removing litter and organic surface layers, depth-inconsistent horizons, samples collected before 1970, out-of-range values, and duplicates. Of 226,821 profiles, 174,092 were retained, including 107,482 with topsoil (0 to 30 cm) SOC, 99,199 with SIC, and 118,807 with pH (table S1). Training profiles are unevenly distributed: North America contributes 36% of topsoil SOC profiles and Asia 5%, and only 28% (SOC) to 41% (SIC) as many profiles reach 100 to 200 cm as 0 to 30 cm (fig. S22 and table S18). Profiles were harmonized to 0 to 30, 30 to 100, and 100 to 200 cm with mass-preserving splines [@bishop99;@malone09]. For each layer, we trained gradient-boosted models [@chen16] for pH; for SIC as a two-step model, first classifying carbonate presence (SIC > 0.1 g kg⁻¹) and then predicting the amount where present, with predicted pH as a covariate; for mineral-soil SOC, with a separate classifier for organic horizons constrained by the global peatland area [@xu18]; and for bulk density. Observations were weighted by the inverse number of profiles in each 0.5° cell to limit the influence of dense survey networks."),
('p', "Under random 10-fold cross-validation, the protocol used by Huang *et al.* [@huang24], the models discriminated carbonate-bearing from carbonate-free soils well [area under the curve (AUC) = 0.95 to 0.97; F1 = 0.73 to 0.86 across depths] and predicted bulk density (R² = 0.77 to 0.81), topsoil mineral SOC (R² = 0.64), and pH (R² = 0.65 to 0.67) with moderate skill (R² is the model-efficiency coefficient; table S3 and fig. S1). The amount of SIC where present was harder to predict (R² = 0.31 to 0.49 on the original scale; 0.41 to 0.62 on the log scale). These scores are below those reported by Huang *et al.* for SIC (AUC ≈ 0.99, R² ≈ 0.79) and pH (R² ≈ 0.85) [@huang24], a gap that we attribute to our smaller covariate set, coarser 10′ training stack, and different observation population. Because random cross-validation is optimistic when observations are spatially clustered [@ploton20;@meyer21], all scores should be read as interpolation skill within the sampled environmental space."),
('p', "Global soils store 4105 Pg of carbon in the upper 2 m (90% CI, 3675 to 4549 Pg; ±265 Pg), comprising 2506 Pg SOC (2263 to 2761; ±151) and 1598 Pg SIC (1408 to 1801; ±119) (Table 1, Fig. 1, fig. S14, and table S5). SIC therefore makes up 39% of total soil carbon, and an SOC-only inventory omits nearly two-fifths of the stock. The two pools are distributed very differently with depth. SOC is spread almost evenly across the three layers (848, 818, and 841 Pg), whereas SIC increases strongly with depth (178, 602, and 819 Pg), so that 89% of SIC, but only 66% of SOC, lies below 30 cm (Fig. 1, A and B; subsoil maps in fig. S9). This depth asymmetry holds on every continent, where 84 to 95% of SIC but 62 to 70% of SOC lies below 30 cm (fig. S17 and table S13). Summed by latitude band, SOC peaks in the boreal zone (near 65°N) and SIC in the northern subtropics (near 30°N) (fig. S16). Relative to the local stock, fold-model spread is small for SOC (median cell coefficient of variation, 7%) and larger for SIC (30%) (fig. S20). Organic horizons in peatlands contribute 361 Pg (14%) of SOC. In the upper 1 m, our SIC estimate (780 Pg) lies within the range of earlier soil-unit-based estimates [695 to 748 Pg [@batjes96]; 940 Pg [@lal99]], and the 0 to 2 m total is close to the WISE30sec-based estimate of 1558 Pg [@plaza18] but about 30% below the 2305 ± 636 Pg of Huang *et al.* [@huang24]; our 90% CI upper bound (1801 Pg) overlaps the lower half of their ±1 SD range (table S6). The 0 to 2 m SOC total agrees with Batjes [2376 to 2456 Pg [@batjes96]] to within about 5%, whereas our topsoil SOC [848 Pg (774 to 924; ±46)] is higher than earlier inventories [684 to 724 Pg [@batjes96]; 658 Pg in SoilGrids on the same land mask [@poggio21]]. Against 15,378 independent LUCAS 2018 topsoil samples [@orgiazzi18], the mean bias is +28.6 Mg C ha⁻¹ (R² = −0.46) when the 0 to 30 cm map is compared with 0 to 20 cm observations and +6.9 Mg C ha⁻¹ (R² ≈ 0.02) after depth harmonization (table S6); we report this residual bias and do not rescale the global maps."),
('p', "SIC and SOC occupy largely complementary territories (Fig. 1 and fig. S23). SIC exceeds 60% of total soil carbon on 26% of land, including the Sahara, the Arabian Peninsula, central Asia, interior Australia, and the western interior of North America, whereas it contributes less than 5% on 40% of land, mainly in humid tropical and boreal regions (fig. S23A). Grouped by Köppen–Geiger climate [@beck23], arid (B) zones hold 1064 Pg of SIC, about two-thirds of the global SIC stock, whereas cold (D) zones hold the most SOC (1060 Pg) (fig. S15 and table S14). Russia, Canada, the United States, and China hold the largest total soil-carbon stocks, but their composition differs, and in several dryland countries, including Saudi Arabia, Iran, and Algeria, SIC is the dominant component of the national total (figs. S23, B and C, and S25, and table S15). Across countries, the SIC share is not predicted by the size of the SOC stock, and roughly a quarter of countries fall in each quadrant defined by the global medians (fig. S23C). A national inventory that tracks SOC alone therefore misses a large, and in some countries the dominant, part of the soil carbon stock."),
('p', "Applying the same models to a hybrid 30 arc sec (~1 km) covariate stack, with climate interpolated from the 10′ grid and elevation at native resolution, gives a topsoil SOC integral (908 Pg) within about 6% of the 10′ integral of the same central model (858 Pg) but a substantially lower topsoil SIC integral (104 versus 185 Pg; fig. S12). Because SIC totals are sensitive to terrain representation in carbonate-rich regions, we use the 10′ training grid for all totals."),

('h', "Climate controls organic carbon directly but inorganic carbon through soil pH"),
('p', "Shapley additive explanations [@lundberg17;@lundberg20] show that the two pools are controlled by different groups of drivers (Fig. 2A and fig. S3). Climate accounts for 66% of the explained variation in mineral SOC and 80% in pH, with the logarithm of the humidity index ln(1 + P/PET), where P is precipitation and PET is potential evapotranspiration, as the leading predictor of both (14% and 26% of mean |SHAP|, respectively). Mineral SOC declines steeply with increasing mean annual temperature up to a breakpoint at −3.8°C and more gradually above it (fig. S5J), in line with the higher temperature sensitivity of soil carbon in cold climates [@koven17]. In contrast, soil properties account for 74% of the explained variation in SIC presence and 55% in SIC amount, overwhelmingly through predicted pH (51% and 33%). Climate therefore affects SOC directly but carbonates mainly indirectly, by setting pH."),
('p', "A standardized path model fitted to 58,404 topsoil observations makes this aridity→pH→SIC pathway explicit (Fig. 2B; full diagram also in fig. S8). The humidity index has a strong negative effect on pH (β = −0.56), pH has a strong positive effect on SIC (β = 0.67), and the direct effect of the humidity index on SIC is negligible (β = 0.03), so that nearly all of the climate effect on SIC (−0.35 in total) is routed through pH (−0.38). The humidity index has, by contrast, a direct positive effect on SOC (β = 0.35). The same water balance therefore pushes the two pools in opposite directions. These descriptive linear paths and the nonlinear model point to the same mechanism: water balance sets pH [@slessarev16], and pH sets whether carbonates can persist [@huang24]."),
('p', "The resulting relations are strongly nonlinear (Fig. 2, E and F, and figs. S4 to S6). In the observations, the probability of carbonate presence follows a logistic function of pH with a midpoint (pH₅₀) of 7.10 (n = 91,273 topsoil samples; fig. S6A), and the median SIC content of carbonate-bearing soils increases by nearly two orders of magnitude between pH 5 and 8.4 before leveling off (fig. S6B). The partial dependence of modeled carbonate presence on pH shows a breakpoint at pH 6.64, above which the probability of presence rises steeply (Fig. 2F). The breakpoint in the partial dependence of pH on the humidity index lies at ln(1 + P/PET) = 0.75 (P/PET ≈ 1.1), beyond which soils converge on an acidic plateau near pH 5.9 (fig. S5L). Holding pH constant, the residual partial dependence of carbonate presence on climate is weak (a change in probability of about 0.03), with breakpoints at P/PET ≈ 0.74 [ln(1 + P/PET) = 0.56] and a mean annual precipitation of 457 mm (Fig. 2E and fig. S5, A and B). The pH breakpoint coincides with the water-balance threshold at which soils acidify [@slessarev16], and the carbonate breakpoint lies close to the conventional dryland boundary (aridity index 0.65). Our occurrence sigmoid is centered at a lower pH than the curve of Huang *et al.* for the pH contribution to SIC amount (steepest between pH 7 and 8.5) [@huang24]; the two are complementary, because carbonates first appear near neutral pH and then accumulate toward calcite equilibrium."),

('h', "Organic losses outweigh carbonate gains, leaving a net loss of total soil carbon"),
('p', "We projected equilibrium changes in SOC and SIC by applying the present-day models to 2081–2100 climates from six CMIP6 models under four shared socioeconomic pathways (SSPs) [@oneill16] while holding soil and terrain covariates fixed (space-for-time substitution). Uncertainty was propagated across 10 random-10-fold fold-models of the final workflow and the six climate models; all maps and headline values come from this one ensemble, and maps are ensemble means whose area integrals equal the headline means (supplementary text S2). Under SSP5-8.5, at equilibrium SOC in the upper 2 m decreases by 378 Pg (90% CI, 254 to 526 Pg; ±91 Pg), whereas SIC increases by 229 Pg (130 to 382 Pg; ±69 Pg; Table 2, Fig. 3B, and table S7). Total soil carbon therefore decreases by 150 Pg (41 to 245 Pg; ±68 Pg). Under SSP1-2.6, SOC decreases by 129 Pg (54 to 240 Pg) and SIC increases by 84 Pg (47 to 140 Pg); TC decreases by 45 Pg, but its 90% CI extends from a decrease of 110 Pg to an increase of 4 Pg, so the direction of the equilibrium net change is not resolved under this scenario, whereas the TC decrease is resolved under SSP2-4.5, SSP3-7.0, and SSP5-8.5 (Table 2). In every scenario, the equilibrium SIC increase offsets only 60 to 65% of the SOC decrease. Climate models contribute more spread than fold-models: under SSP5-8.5, the GCM-mean SOC decrease ranges from 271 Pg (MPI-ESM1-2-HR) to 512 Pg (UKESM1-0-LL), yet every GCM, averaged over fold-models, projects that SOC decreases, SIC increases, and TC decreases under every scenario (fig. S18 and table S16)."),
('p', "The two pools mostly move in opposite directions, consistent with a trade-off rather than a co-loss. SOC decreases are widespread: equilibrium topsoil SOC decreases on 67% (SSP1-2.6) to 75% (SSP5-8.5) of mapped land (Fig. 3A), and losses are concentrated in boreal and cold-temperate regions of North America and Eurasia (Fig. 3C). SIC increases are concentrated in Europe, northern and central Asia, and the western and central United States, whereas SIC decreases in India, the Arabian Peninsula, the Sahel, the eastern United States, and the Canadian Prairies, where projected changes in water balance lower pH (Fig. 3D). This geography resembles the projections of Huang *et al.*, with the largest topsoil SIC losses in Saudi Arabia and India and gains in Russia, the United States, Spain, and France [@huang24]. Under SSP5-8.5, SOC decreases while SIC increases (trade-off) on 69 Mkm², or 50% of the 137.3 Mkm² of mapped land, whereas both pools decrease (co-loss) on 16%, both increase on 20%, and SOC increases while SIC decreases on 13% (Fig. 3E and table S8). The trade-off is the largest class under every scenario and expands with warming (41% of mapped land under SSP1-2.6), whereas co-loss contracts (21% under SSP1-2.6) (table S19). Under SSP5-8.5, at least 90% of the 60 ensemble members agree that SOC decreases on 41% of mapped land and that SIC increases on 43% (fig. S19, which also maps ΔTC under all four scenarios), and 67% of countries fall in the trade-off quadrant (fig. S26). At the continental scale, Europe and North America account for most of the SOC decrease, whereas Europe and Asia account for most of the SIC increase (Fig. 3F and table S13)."),
('p', "Shifts in Köppen–Geiger climate zones [@beck23] by 2071–2099 affect 7.2 Mkm² (5.3% of mapped land) under SSP1-2.6 and 19.1 Mkm² (13.9%) under SSP5-8.5 (table S8). Under SSP5-8.5, on land that changes climate zone SOC decreases by 61 Pg and SIC increases by 44 Pg, a net TC decrease of 17 Pg. The direction depends on the transition (fig. S21 and table S17). On land that changes from cold to temperate (D→C; 4.9 Mkm², the largest transition), SOC decreases by 22 Pg (17 to 27 Pg) and SIC increases by 34 Pg (24 to 57 Pg), and SOC likewise decreases while SIC increases on land that becomes arid (D→B, C→B, and A→B). On land that changes from polar to cold (E→D; 4.2 Mkm²), by contrast, both pools decrease: SIC by 9 Pg (4 to 14 Pg) and SOC by 18 Pg (90% CI from a decrease of 39 Pg to an increase of 2 Pg); on land that changes from temperate to tropical (C→A; 4.0 Mkm²), SOC decreases by 5 Pg (3.5 to 6.9 Pg) and the SIC change is not resolved. Shifted land carries only 16% of the global SOC decrease and 19% of the SIC increase, so most of the redistribution occurs within existing climate zones, through gradual changes in water balance, rather than at shifting zone boundaries."),
('p', "Equilibrium projections describe where the system would settle, not how quickly. To estimate changes realized by 2100, we applied a first-order response, Δ(t) = Δ_eq[1 − exp(−t/τ)], with t = 85 years and turnover times τ sampled from log-uniform ranges informed by radiocarbon and turnover syntheses [@carvalhais14;@he16;@shi20]: 20 to 100 years for SOC at 0 to 30 cm, 100 to 1000 years at 30 to 100 cm, 500 to 3000 years at 100 to 200 cm, and 1000 to 10,000 years for SIC at all depths (materials and methods). These ranges are assumptions chosen to bracket published estimates rather than to fit our data."),
('p', "Under these constraints, the SIC compensation nearly vanishes (Fig. 4 and table S7). By 2100 under SSP5-8.5, SOC decreases by 188 Pg (105 to 292 Pg), whereas SIC increases by only 7 Pg (2 to 19 Pg), about 3% of its equilibrium increase. Total soil carbon therefore decreases by 180 Pg (100 to 282 Pg) by 2100. This realized TC decrease is larger than the equilibrium decrease (150 Pg) because the SIC increase that offsets part of the SOC decrease at equilibrium is realized far more slowly; beyond 2100, SOC would continue to decrease and SIC to increase, so TC would partly recover toward the equilibrium value (Table 3). TC decreases by 2100 under every scenario: by 54 Pg (18 to 116 Pg) under SSP1-2.6, 97 Pg (51 to 182 Pg) under SSP2-4.5, 144 Pg (74 to 245 Pg) under SSP3-7.0, and 180 Pg under SSP5-8.5, and the 90% CIs exclude zero in every case. Because turnover is fastest near the surface, most of the realized SOC decrease under SSP5-8.5 occurs in the upper 30 cm (147 Pg; 81 to 238 Pg). The magnitude of these realized changes depends on τ, but their direction does not. The asymmetry has a simple physical basis. Organic matter is lost through microbial respiration, which responds to temperature and moisture within years, whereas pedogenic carbonate accumulation requires a supply of calcium and magnesium from weathering or dust and slow precipitation in a drying profile [@zamanian16;@monger15]. An equilibrium SIC gain is therefore a long-term potential, not a near-term sink."),

('h', "Nitrogen-driven acidification erodes the carbonate compensation"),
('p', "Carbonate loss under acidification, unlike carbonate gain, can be rapid [@kim20;@song22]. Huang *et al.* estimated that nitrogen-induced acidification would remove 10, 14, and 23 Pg of topsoil SIC over 2020–2050 under three INMS nitrogen-policy scenarios projected with the GLOBIOM model [@huang24]. We applied the same empirical relation between cumulative fertilizer nitrogen input (N_in, Mg N ha⁻¹) and pH change derived from repeated Chinese soil surveys, ΔpH = −0.06 N_in + 0.11 [@huang24;@guo10], to cropland fertilizer projections from the IMAGE model distributed through ISIMIP3b [@stehfest14;@lange19], and converted ΔpH into ΔSIC through finite differences of our SIC model with all other covariates held fixed (materials and methods). GLOBIOM/INMS fields were not obtained; SSP2-4.5 inputs were interpolated. Counting only cells that lose carbonate (loss-only totals), these IMAGE inputs decrease topsoil SIC by 1.6, 1.8, 2.1, and 2.2 Pg over 2020–2050 under SSP1-2.6, SSP2-4.5, SSP3-7.0, and SSP5-8.5, respectively, and SIC in the upper 2 m by 13.4 to 17.4 Pg (Fig. 5 and table S9); including the cells in which the positive intercept raises pH (net totals), SIC decreases by 0.9 to 1.6 Pg in the topsoil and 7.0 to 11.1 Pg in the upper 2 m. Losses are largest in the United States, China, India, and Pakistan (Fig. 5B)."),
('p', "Nitrogen and climate therefore push SIC in opposite directions: climate change raises mean topsoil pH (by 0.1 to 0.3 units across scenarios; table S16) and increases SIC, whereas fertilizer-driven acidification lowers pH and decreases SIC. The two estimates come from separate experiments over different horizons (nitrogen, 2020–2050; climate, realized by 2100) and are not additive, but their relative size sets the direction of SIC change (Table 3 and table S19). At equilibrium, the climate-driven SIC increase (229 Pg under SSP5-8.5) is more than 10 times the nitrogen-driven decrease in the upper 2 m (17.4 Pg loss-only; 11.1 Pg net), so SIC would still increase. By 2100, however, the realized climate-driven SIC increase (2.6 to 7.4 Pg across scenarios) is smaller than the central nitrogen-driven decrease over 2020–2050 alone (7.0 to 11.1 Pg net; 13.4 to 17.4 Pg loss-only), so the central estimates imply that SIC decreases this century. This direction is robust to the τ uncertainty of the realized increase under SSP1-2.6 (95th percentile, 6.6 Pg, below both nitrogen totals) and, for loss-only totals, under SSP2-4.5 and SSP3-7.0; under SSP5-8.5 the 95th percentile of the realized increase (19 Pg) exceeds both nitrogen totals, so the direction of SIC change by 2100 is not resolved. The 0 to 2 m nitrogen totals also assume that the topsoil pH change applies at all depths. In every case TC decreases, because the realized SOC decrease by 2100 (57 to 188 Pg across scenarios) is several times larger than either SIC term. Our topsoil estimates are several-fold to about an order of magnitude smaller than those of Huang *et al.*, whose topsoil losses alone (10 to 23 Pg) would exceed the central realized SIC increase under every scenario; simple ratios of their 10, 14, and 23 Pg targets to our IMAGE losses are roughly 6 to 11 (table S9). These are ratios of losses, not rerun scenarios, and indicate only approximately how much larger nitrogen inputs would have to be. Adding atmospheric deposition raises topsoil losses by only a few tenths of a petagram, and a stoichiometric approach [0.21 kg C per kg N; range 0.055 to 0.32 [@zamanian18]] gives about 0.8 to 0.9 Pg. The discrepancy therefore arises mainly from the nitrogen trajectory, not from the carbonate map. The ΔpH relation was calibrated on intensively fertilized Chinese croplands [@guo10], and its positive intercept implies alkalinization below 1.8 Mg N ha⁻¹; global estimates should therefore be reported with the nitrogen trajectory that produces them."),

('h', "Particulate carbon carries the near-term organic loss; mineral-associated carbon carries the committed loss"),
('p', "To resolve which organic pools carry the SOC loss, we modeled the MAOC fraction of mineral-soil SOC (f_MAOC) from 1005 harmonized particle-size and density-fractionation measurements [@georgiou22;@georgiou_data] (fig. S2). After removing records in which POC was not measured, the model reached a random 10-fold cross-validated R² of 0.58 (five repeats, 0.57 ± 0.02; RMSE = 0.129; fig. S1B). Unlike SOC itself, f_MAOC is controlled more by texture than by climate: clay content, silt content, and cation exchange capacity show breakpoints at 22%, 32%, and 25 cmol kg⁻¹, respectively (fig. S5, E to G), consistent with mineral protection of organic matter [@rasmussen18;@georgiou22]. In mineral soils, MAOC stores 518 Pg (90% CI, 473 to 565; ±28) and POC 183 Pg (163 to 205; ±13) in the upper 30 cm, and MAOC stores 1048 Pg (946 to 1152; ±62) in the upper 1 m (Fig. 6, A, C, and D; comparison with Georgiou et al. in fig. S24), with an area-mean MAOC fraction of about 0.72 in topsoils. Our 0 to 1 m MAOC estimate lies between the 899 Pg published by Georgiou *et al.* for non-permafrost mineral soils and the 1133 Pg obtained by summing their gridded product over all land [@georgiou22]. Subsoil estimates rest on only 34 fraction records below 30 cm and are indicative (fig. S10)."),
('p', "Under SSP5-8.5, at equilibrium topsoil MAOC decreases by 78 Pg (38 to 124 Pg) and POC by 39 Pg (22 to 60 Pg), so that the slow pool loses twice as much as the fast pool (Fig. 6B, fig. S7, and table S10). With τ sampled log-uniformly within literature ranges, by 2100 MAOC decreases by 37 Pg (12 to 79 Pg) and POC by 39 Pg (21 to 60 Pg): POC realizes essentially its entire equilibrium decrease this century, whereas MAOC realizes about half and carries the larger committed decrease (41 Pg) beyond 2100. Timing therefore reverses the ranking of the two fractions: MAOC carries the larger equilibrium decrease, but by 2100 both have decreased by similar amounts. Both fractions decrease under every scenario, although under SSP1-2.6 the 90% CI of the MAOC change includes zero (table S10). The near-term loss is therefore expressed first in the fast, input-responsive POC pool, while the slow MAOC pool, often considered the most persistent store [@lavallee20;@georgiou22], carries a delayed loss that will continue to be realized after emissions are reduced. MAOC and POC are both below their global medians on 43% of land, mainly in drylands (Fig. 6E), where SIC makes up much of the soil carbon stock. These projections apply to mineral soils and are not directly additive with the SOC projections above, because they use a separate fraction model and exclude organic horizons (supplementary text S2)."),

('h', "Net soil carbon change is organic loss minus inorganic compensation"),
('p', "Following total soil carbon through both pools changes the answer (Table 3). Under SSP5-8.5, the budget at equilibrium is a climate-driven SOC decrease of 378 Pg, a climate-driven SIC increase of 229 Pg, and a net TC decrease of 150 Pg; by 2100 it is an SOC decrease of 188 Pg, an SIC increase of 7 Pg, and a net TC decrease of 180 Pg. Nitrogen-driven acidification adds a separate SIC decrease (11 to 17 Pg in the upper 2 m over 2020–2050, net and loss-only) that is not included in these totals because it comes from a different experiment and horizon; if added, it would enlarge the TC decrease and could reverse the direction of the small realized SIC change, but not the direction of TC. An SOC-only assessment therefore overstates the equilibrium decrease of total soil carbon (378 versus 150 Pg under SSP5-8.5), because it ignores the offsetting carbonate increase. Ignoring SIC, or treating its equilibrium gain as a sink, understates the risk: the gain is realized only slowly, nitrogen-driven acidification can remove carbonate faster than climate builds it, and a pool that holds 39% of soil carbon, mostly below 30 cm, goes unmonitored. On the century scale the SIC increase is small, so the net change in soil carbon is close to the SOC decrease itself, and total soil carbon decreases (soils are a net carbon source) this century under all four scenarios, adding to the climate–carbon feedbacks assessed by the IPCC [@canadell21]. Carbonate gains should not be counted as an offset for SOC losses in national inventories or in projections that inform nationally determined contributions (NDCs)."),
('p', "The MAOC and POC split determines the persistence of that loss. Near-term losses fall mainly on POC, which is reversible in principle through increased organic inputs, whereas MAOC carries a committed loss that will continue to be realized after the climate stabilizes. Inventory depth matters too. The IPCC Tier 1 method for soil carbon stock change considers SOC in the upper 30 cm of mineral soils [@ipcc06;@ipcc19], which in our inventory contains 34% of SOC and 11% of SIC. Inventories and soil-carbon monitoring, reporting, and verification schemes, including those supporting soil-carbon crediting [@minasny17], should report SIC and deeper layers at least in carbonate-bearing regions and should distinguish fast (POC) from slow (MAOC) organic pools."),
('p', "Management priorities differ between the two kinds of soil. In carbonate-bearing drylands, the priority is to protect SIC from acidification. IPCC inventory guidance accounts for CO₂ from applied agricultural lime [@ipcc06], but not for the dissolution of native carbonates driven by fertilizer acidification [@zamanian18;@raza20]. Reducing nitrogen surpluses limits this loss, and liming can counteract acidification, although its net CO₂ balance depends on whether dissolved carbonate is exported as bicarbonate or released as CO₂ [@hamilton07;@west05]. Enhanced rock weathering on croplands also interacts with soil pH and carbonate equilibria [@beerling20]. Because the most vulnerable soils lie near the pH threshold we identified (pH ≈ 6.6 to 7.1), maps of that threshold could help target nitrogen and liming management. In humid and cold regions, where SIC contributes little and SOC losses are concentrated, the priority is to maintain organic inputs that sustain the fast POC pool, while recognizing that the committed MAOC loss cannot be offset quickly."),
('p', "Our analysis has important limitations (supplementary text S6). Space-for-time substitution assumes that present-day climate–carbon relationships hold in future and ignores land-use change, irrigation, dust inputs, and elevated-CO₂ effects on soil pCO₂. The first-order time-limited response is a simple bridge, not a process model, and its τ values are assumptions. Our covariate set lacks vegetation and nitrogen-deposition predictors, so drivers acting through these pathways cannot be attributed. Random cross-validation overstates out-of-sample skill in sparsely sampled regions [@ploton20;@meyer21]. Topsoil SOC retains a modest positive bias against LUCAS, and our SIC total remains below that of Huang *et al.*, although our CI overlaps the lower half of their ±1 SD. Nitrogen trajectories rely on IMAGE fertilizer with an interpolated SSP2-4.5. These caveats affect magnitudes but not the central asymmetry: carbonate gains are slow, organic losses are fast, and crediting equilibrium carbonate gains against near-term organic losses is not justified on policy-relevant timescales."),
('p', "Joint accounting of SOC and SIC thus changes the question from how much carbon soils hold to how quickly each form responds. The net global change in soil carbon is the SOC decrease minus the SIC increase, and the nature of the SOC loss, fast and reversible in POC or slow and committed in MAOC, determines how long it will last. Sharpening these estimates will require repeated measurements of both pools at the same sites, better constraints on carbonate formation rates, and nitrogen scenarios from several land-use models. Until then, the most defensible reading of the evidence is that total soil carbon will decrease over this century, and that the inorganic pool, though large and increasing at equilibrium, will not compensate for organic losses on timescales relevant to climate policy."),
]

METHODS_SUMMARY = [
"Soil profiles from WoSIS [@batjes20] were screened following Huang *et al.* [@huang24] (table S1) and harmonized to 0–30, 30–100, and 100–200 cm with equal-area splines [@bishop99;@malone09]. SIC was computed as 0.12 × calcium carbonate equivalent. Thirty-two covariates (19 WorldClim bioclimatic variables [@fick17], Hargreaves PET [@hargreaves85] and ln(1 + P/PET), elevation, slope, topographic position, SoilGrids clay, sand, silt, and cation exchange capacity [@poggio21] (one map per property, used for all three layers rather than depth-weighted), and GLiM lithology [@hartmann12] as a class code plus three indicators) were assembled on a 10′ grid (table S2); predicted pH was added as a 33rd covariate for SIC. For each layer, XGBoost [@chen16] models were trained for pH, SIC (presence classifier plus log-amount regression with smearing correction [@duan83] and predicted pH as covariate), mineral SOC with an organic-horizon classifier scaled to PEATMAP [@xu18], and bulk density, with 0.5° declustering weights. Hyperparameters were selected and accuracy assessed by random 10-fold cross-validation (tables S3 and S4); R² is the model-efficiency coefficient. Stocks were computed as concentration × bulk density × (1 − coarse fraction) × thickness, with the coarse fraction set to the per-layer median of harmonized WoSIS observations. Present-day uncertainty combines 10 fold-model spread with Monte Carlo perturbations of bulk density, coarse fragments, effective thickness, and organic C density (90% CI and ±1 SD). MAOC fractions were modeled from 1005 measurements [@georgiou22] (table S12). Future climates (2081–2100; six CMIP6 models; SSP1-2.6 to SSP5-8.5) were applied with the delta method; equilibrium and time-limited changes use the same 10 fold-models × 6 GCMs ensemble (maps = ensemble means so that map integrals equal headline means). Nitrogen-driven ΔpH followed ΔpH = −0.06 N_in + 0.11 [@huang24] with ISIMIP3b IMAGE fertilizer inputs (SSP2-4.5 interpolated; GLOBIOM not obtained). Drivers were attributed with TreeSHAP [@lundberg20], partial dependence with two-segment breakpoints, observed pH–SIC logistic fits, and a standardized path model. Continental, climate-zone, and national totals integrate the same 10′ maps (tables S13 to S15). Changes (Δ) are future minus present; negative values denote a decrease of the soil stock (loss) and positive values an increase (gain). In the text, changes are written as decreases or increases with 90% CIs given as magnitudes in the same direction; tables and figures give signed values, except columns explicitly labeled as losses. All stocks and changes are in Pg C (10¹⁵ g C; written Pg in the text), and land-area shares refer to the 137.3 Mkm² of mapped land. Full details are given in the supplementary materials.",
]

ACK = 'We thank ISRIC – World Soil Information for WoSIS, the European Commission Joint Research Centre for LUCAS 2018 topsoil data, K. Georgiou and co-authors for the open fraction synthesis, the WorldClim, SoilGrids, GLiM, Köppen–Geiger, CMIP6, and ISIMIP communities for open data, and Y. Huang and co-authors for detailed supplementary methods that made this comparison possible. **Funding:** [to be completed]. **Author contributions:** [to be completed]. **Competing interests:** The authors declare no competing interests. **Data and materials availability:** All input datasets are publicly available from the sources cited. Global maps (10′ and 30 arc sec), model objects, analysis code, and the numerical results underlying all figures will be deposited in a public repository [DOI to be assigned on acceptance].'

