LEGENDS = {
1: ('Present-day soil organic and inorganic carbon stocks.', '(A and B) Probability density of SOC (A) and SIC (B) stocks per 10′ grid cell (log₁₀ Mg C ha⁻¹) for 0 to 30, 30 to 100, and 100 to 200 cm; dashed lines mark layer medians. The inset in (A) shows the share of each layer in the 0 to 200 cm total for SOC and SIC (SOC: 34, 33, and 34%; SIC: 11, 38, and 51%). (C and D) Topsoil (0 to 30 cm) SOC (C) and SIC (D) stocks (Mg C ha⁻¹); stacked insets give the share of the global topsoil stock by continent (AF, Africa; AS, Asia; EU, Europe; NA, North America; OC, Oceania; SA, South America). (E and F) SOC (E) and SIC (F) stocks in 0 to 200 cm; insets give the proportion of land area in each class. Grey, not mapped (ice, Antarctica, or missing covariates). Global totals (10′ grid; mean with 90% CI and ±1 SD): SOC 848 (774–924; ±46), 818, and 841 Pg and SIC 178 (163–194; ±9), 602, and 819 Pg for the three layers, or 2506 (2263–2761; ±151) Pg SOC and 1598 (1408–1801; ±119) Pg SIC in 0 to 200 cm (TC 4105; 3675–4549; ±265). Maps are shown on the 10′ analysis grid in Robinson projection; 30 arc sec products are shown in fig. S12; present-day uncertainty maps in fig. S14. National SIC/TC geography and country rankings are in figs. S23 and S25.'),
2: ('Drivers of soil pH, inorganic carbon, and organic carbon, and the aridity–pH–SIC pathway.', '(A) Share of mean absolute SHAP value (%) attributed to climate, soil, topography, parent material, and sampling covariates (depth and fractionation method; f_MAOC only) for pH, SIC presence, SIC amount, mineral SOC, and f_MAOC. Exact TreeSHAP values were computed for 30,000 randomly selected land cells. (B) Standardized path model (n = 58,404 topsoil observations) with path coefficients on arrows (blue, positive; red, negative); thickness scales with |β|. Indirect aridity→pH→SIC = −0.38. (C and D) Dominant driver group per grid cell for SIC presence (C) and mineral SOC (D); insets give the proportion of land area per group. (E and F) Partial dependence of the probability of SIC presence (SIC > 0.1 g kg⁻¹, topsoil) on ln(1 + P/PET) (E) and predicted pH (F). Dashed lines mark breakpoints of two-segment piecewise-linear fits [ln(1 + P/PET) = 0.56, or P/PET ≈ 0.74; pH 6.6], with breakpoint values labeled above the axes. Vegetation and nitrogen-deposition covariates were not included in the models and cannot be attributed. Dominant-driver maps for pH, SIC amount, and f_MAOC, and f_MAOC partial-dependence panels, are shown in figs. S11 and S5.'),
3: ('Divergent equilibrium responses and spatial trade-offs under climate change.', '(A) Probability density of the equilibrium change in topsoil (0 to 30 cm) SOC per grid cell under four SSPs (2081–2100 relative to the WorldClim 1970–2000 baseline); dashed lines mark medians, and the inset gives the percentage of land area losing (<0) or gaining (>0) SOC. (B) Probability density of the equilibrium change in SOC and SIC (0 to 200 cm) under SSP5-8.5. (C and D) Equilibrium ΔSOC (C) and ΔSIC (D) in 0 to 200 cm under SSP5-8.5 (Mg C ha⁻¹); insets give the proportion of land area in each class. (E) Bivariate map of equilibrium ΔSOC and ΔSIC (0 to 200 cm, ensemble mean of 10 fold-models × 6 GCMs). Colorblind-safe quadrants: SOC decreases while SIC increases (orange; trade-off), both decrease (mauve; co-loss), both increase (blue), and SOC increases while SIC decreases (light blue). The inset gives the share of the 137.3 Mkm² of mapped land in each quadrant: trade-off 50%, co-loss 16%, both increase 20%, SOC↑SIC↓ 13%. Cells with an ensemble-mean change of exactly zero (0.04 Mkm²) are counted with the decreasing class. (F) Continental totals of ΔSOC and ΔSIC (Pg C). Accounting: ensemble-mean fields sum to the headline means (−378 Pg SOC; +229 Pg SIC under SSP5-8.5). Country-level ΔSOC versus ΔSIC scatter in fig. S26.'),
4: ('Kinetic decoupling: equilibrium versus realized change by 2100.', '(A) Ensemble-mean changes in SOC, SIC, and total carbon (TC) in 0 to 200 cm under four SSPs: equilibrium (dark bars) versus first-order time-limited response by 2100 (light bars; τ sampled log-uniform within literature ranges; t = 85 years). Error bars, 90% CI across the 10 fold-models × 6 GCMs ensemble. (B) Fraction of the SSP5-8.5 equilibrium change realized by 2100: SOC ≈ 50%, SIC ≈ 3%. Annotated net ΔTC by 2100 = −180 Pg C. Because SIC compensation is barely realized this century while about half of the SOC loss is, the realized TC decrease (−180 Pg) exceeds the equilibrium TC decrease (−150 Pg).'),
5: ('Nitrogen-driven soil acidification and SIC loss, 2020–2050.', '(A) Probability density of the change in cropland pH from cumulative fertilizer nitrogen input (N_in) over 2020–2050, computed as ΔpH = −0.06 N_in + 0.11 (Huang et al.) with ISIMIP3b IMAGE fertilizer projections; dashed lines mark medians. Positive values arise where N_in < 1.8 Mg N ha⁻¹ because of the positive intercept. (B) The 12 countries with the largest SIC loss (0 to 200 cm, Tg C) under SSP5-8.5, colored by continent. (C and D) Change in topsoil SIC (ΔSIC_N, 0 to 30 cm; Mg C ha⁻¹; negative = decrease) under SSP1-2.6 (C) and SSP5-8.5 (D); insets give the share of the global topsoil loss by continent. (E) Global SIC loss (decrease, shown as a positive magnitude) in 0 to 30 cm (dark bars) and 0 to 200 cm (light bars); error bars, 5th to 95th percentiles across the 10 fold-models. Accounting: maps (C and D) report topsoil loss-only values and panels (B) and (E, light bars) loss-only totals over 0 to 200 cm (cells with ΔSIC > 0 set to zero); net totals including alkalinizing cells are given in table S9. Topsoil (0 to 30 cm) loss-only values are 1.6, 1.8, 2.1, and 2.2 Pg under SSP1-2.6, SSP2-4.5, SSP3-7.0, and SSP5-8.5 (SSP2-4.5 IMAGE fertilizer interpolated as the mean of SSP1-2.6 and SSP3-7.0; GLOBIOM/INMS not obtained), compared with the 10, 14, and 23 Pg of Huang et al. (table S9).'),
6: ('Mineral-associated and particulate organic carbon.', '(A) Probability density of MAOC and POC stocks (log₁₀ Mg C ha⁻¹) in 0 to 30 and 30 to 100 cm of mineral soils; dashed and dotted lines mark medians. (B) Change in topsoil (0 to 30 cm) MAOC and POC by 2081–2100 under four SSPs: equilibrium (dark bars) and time-limited by 2100 (light bars; τ sampled log-uniform within literature ranges). Under SSP5-8.5, ΔMAOC −78 Pg (eq.) / −37 Pg (by 2100) and ΔPOC −39 Pg (eq. and by 2100). (C) Fraction of mineral-soil SOC held as MAOC (f_MAOC, %) in 0 to 30 cm; the inset shows the proportion of land area in each class. (D) Topsoil MAOC stock (Mg C ha⁻¹); the inset gives the share of the global stock by continent. (E) Bivariate map of MAOC and POC stocks split at their global medians; the inset gives the percentage of land area per quadrant. Organic horizons (146 Pg in 0 to 30 cm) are excluded from MAOC and POC. Subsoil fractions are constrained by only 34 measurements and are indicative. Comparison with the Georgiou et al. gridded product is shown in fig. S24.'),
}
